  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the social services law, in relation to increasing access to\nnon-invasive prenatal testing under medicaid",
    "basePrintNo": "S8157",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8157-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §365-k, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8157\n\nSPONSOR: CLEARE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to increasing\naccess to non-invasive prenatal testing under medicaid\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nAmends the social services law to include language that removes limits\non eligibility for prenatal testing\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill removes an unnecessary age restriction on access to non-inva-\nsive prenatal testing (NIPT) for Medicaid patients. Adjusting our Medi-\ncaid program to cover these tests will bring New York's Medicaid cover-\nage in line with the practices of most commercial insurance plans, as\nwell as the practices of a number of other state's Medicaid programs. It\nis also recommended by the American College of Obstetricians and Gyne-\ncologists (AGOG).\n\nThe current Department of Health policy is based on the increasing\nprevalence of trisomy 21 (Down Syndrome) in babies born to women 30 and\nolder. However, this current practice of considering only prevalence and\nnot the number of live births provides an incomplete picture. Although\nthe individual risk of having a child with Down Syndrome increases with\nmaternal age, more children with Down Syndrome are born to younger women\nbecause of the higher birth rate in the younger population.\n\nIf the intention of providing NIPT is to maximize the identification of\nfetuses with Down Syndrome, then the best practice is to offer the\nscreening to all pregnant patients, not only to patients 30 and older.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNEW.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nApril 1st, succeeding.\n",
